 "standard_text": "1.3C compose 10 with two or more addends with and without concrete objects",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 1 making 10 worksheets for Texas TEKS 1.3C: ten frames, pairs and three numbers that make 10, make a ten first and stories. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Find the partner that makes 10.",
 "key_sub": "1 + 9, 2 + 8, 3 + 7, 4 + 6, 5 + 5",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Pairs that make 10: 1 + 9, 2 + 8, 3 + 7, 4 + 6, 5 + 5.</li><li>On a <b>ten frame</b>, the empty boxes show how many more make 10.</li><li>Three numbers can make 10 too: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10.</li><li>When adding three numbers, find two that make 10 first.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>7 dots on a ten frame</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3 boxes are empty</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 7 + 3 = 10</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Counting the dots instead of the empty boxes</td><td>How many more means count the empty boxes.</td></tr><tr><td>Picking a pair that makes 9 or 11</td><td>Add the pair to check.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting one addend</td><td>Add all three numbers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does TEKS 1.3C ask?",
   "a": "Compose 10 with two or more addends with and without concrete objects."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is making 10 useful?",
   "a": "Adding to 10 is easy, so it helps with bigger facts."
  },
  {
   "q": "How many pairs make 10?",
   "a": "1 + 9, 2 + 8, 3 + 7, 4 + 6, 5 + 5 and their turn-arounds."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can three numbers make 10?",
   "a": "Yes, like 2 + 3 + 5."
  }
